(1678798, 223730, 14386, '<td valign="top">NO EXCUSES for comments below.  Well, I guess I just know how to adapt my style.<br/> <br/>Actually, if it is a ''fashion" photography, I do smoothen the face using neatimage.  I also clone out imperfections.  However, if I intend it to be a gritty image, I leave it as it is...even make it more grungy.  As far as having my work recognized, it can work both ways.  The 2 1''s explain it.  <br/> <br/>If you want un-smoothen face...documentary style of photography, I would like to plug my site.  Please click the link underneath and please leave some comments.  I am very proud of this gallery.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/manny_librodo/nanbw" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/manny_librodo/nanbw</a> <br/> <br/>Many thanks for the comments again.  And btw, here are some OUTTAKES from this session.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.pbase.com/manny_librodo/students" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.pbase.com/manny_librodo/students</a> <br/> <br/>Many many thanks again.<br/> <br/>Manny<br/> <br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by christie3:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Kaizen:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by frumoaznicul:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Artyste:</b></div><hr/><i> Over smoothed, oversharpened, and yet still an 8.1<br/> <br/>Unbelievable.  Guess that''s the prize for being a recognizable and very well liked artist on this site.<br/> <br/>Of course, compositionally it is as great as ever, although I''d have loved to have seen a more colorful background. The black seems to really point out the over-sharpening where a more natural background may have helped hide it a little more.   Also, once again, would that I had a bank of "exotic" looking people to shoot.. seems to really make a punch with North Americans. <br/> <br/>Anyway, for me, a touch less of smoothing and sharpening would give this photo some real life.. <br/>Guess that''s why I usually don''t score well here. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>As I also commented during challenge, I completely agree with everything Artyste says. It''s a pitty that this site has become a "whoever is the most popular in the forums" wins. No offence intended to librodo, and nothing against him, it is the only image I commented in the challenge, that means it clearly triggered my attention, I really feel the image would be wonderful with regardless how much noise. Like this it looks like the poor girl is wrapped in a plastic bag, a manequin a doll anything but human. You have true talent it is obvious and it''s a pitty that you ruin your work just to please the DPC forums audience.<br/> <br/>However congrats for the ribbon. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Agreed. <br/> <br/>"Nothing fails like success."<br/> <br/>JV </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I agree as well...And no offense at ALL to Manny, please don''t get me wrong.  I just think that, more often than not, if someone recognizes a popular photog''s work here on DPC, they will give it a high score, regardless of whether or not it is terribly creative or well done.  <br/> <br/>*shrugs*<br/> <br/>But yes.  Congratulationa on the ribbon, you do rock, librodo!  : ) </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'NO EXCUSES for comments below.  Well, I guess I just know how to adapt my style. Actually, if it is a ''fashion" photography, I do smoothen the face using neatimage.  I also clone out imperfections.  However, if I intend it to be a gritty image, I leave it as it is...even make it more grungy.  As far as having my work recognized, it can work both ways.  The 2 1''s explain it.   If you want un-smoothen face...documentary style of photography, I would like to plug my site.  Please click the link